rupt people and they say the  president still has not done anything  about them  do is it possible to gauge the degree of  support that kais saeed has  not  easily no there were protests  in support of him a few days ago over  the weekend when there were other  protests  against what he's done there are other  protests planned possibly for this  weekend on sunday for people supporting  him as well  so you can  there was a there was a belief  when he took over the reigns of power  when he suspended parliament two months  ago  there seemed to be a general opinion  that people were very frustrated with  the democratically elected parliament  very frustrated with nothing being done  the economy was in a lot of trouble  and that had been exacerbated by the  pandemic and so there was people felt  that something needed to be done and  that is why it seems over the past two  months  the president has been given time  uh by these civil society groups and by  these political parties to offer some  sort of solution to the crisis tunisia  faces it finds itself in but so far the  president hasn't done that and this is  why people's patience seems to be  beginning to run out  all right burner thanks for that uh  bernard smith there in tunis  let's speak now to fadil alireza who's  the editor-in-chief of michikal which is  a tunisian news and analyst analysis  website joins us from tunis  also fadil first of all bernie was  saying our correspondent there was  saying that uh the president has been  given time but now opposition parties  are running out of patience is that how  you see it  certainly that's true um we saw um not  only immediately the the main governing  parties that were um saw them themselves  lose power when he first parliament on  july 25th opposing the president's moves  um we actually have seen uh other  parties as well that initially were  maybe on the fence that were sort of  tepidly um uh  or maybe skeptically uh supportive of  the july 25th move turn against