ent from from  moving forward on many issues you can  also see the business community as  working together alongside it's not  often that you see uh the union of  workers working together with the  business community represented by the  main chamber of commerce the utica so  the fact that there's these groups uh  sort of co-electing together and seeing  that they'd like to see some answers  from the president suggest that he's  going to have a difficult time going  forward with his plans if he continues  to go alone  it's a question i asked him bernard the  correspondent ben smith  is it possible to gauge do you think the  degree of support that the president has  and clearly he had support at the  beginning but  as we said people are running out of  patience can you can you gauge that can  you tell how many people what kind of  division in percentage terms there is  it's very difficult we saw early polling  at the very uh beginning stages that  showed a widespread support for the  president certainly when he made a  decision uh you know people went into  the streets across the country on mass  you know at night breaking curfew um  since then we've seen that uh um seem to  deteriorate certainly some of his  supporters have become a little bit less  vociferous on social media we've also  seen sort of counter protesters these  last two demonstrations against the  president um  getting smaller in number um but we'll  see you know there's also always a  possibility that the president will be  able to mobilize his supporters  particularly if he um finds a particular  issue that he can use to mobilize them  uh in the past he's really um focused on  anti-corruption issues as the  correspondent uh mentioned earlier um we  haven't seen quite a lot tangible in  terms of his anti-corruption efforts um  if he doesn't produce uh soon that may  also eat into his support among his base  right interesting thanks very much  indeed for that uh editor-in-chief of  mexico joining us fr